, col-1) would be? Actually our diagonal direction vector is (-1, +1). So starting from D12, adding (-1,+1) gives C13? That’s (12-1=11, D+1=E) wait no: D12 + (-1,+1) = (11, E) which is E11, that’s already part of the three. The other direction: from F10 adding (1,-1) gives (11, E) again. So the line is actually two-ended? Actually a line with direction (-1,1) means points (r, c) such that r = r0 - k, c = c0 + k. For F10: r0=10, c0=F. For E11: r=11, c=E. That’s r = r0+1, c = c0-1? Wait, 11 = 10+1, E = F-1. So that’s direction (+1,-1). So the diagonal is actually direction (+1,-1) (down-left). Let’s recalc: F10 (10,F), E11 (11,E), D12 (12,D). So each step row+1, col-1. So direction is (+1,-1). Then upward would be row-1, col+1: from F10 that’s G9 (9,G) which is X. Downward from D12 would be C13? D12 (12,D) + (+1,-1) = (13,C) which is C13, and that’s X. So both ends of the three are blocked by X! So it’s a blocked three, not an open three. That’s not a direct winning threat.\n\nSo White has no immediate four or open three. Need to look for creating a four or open three. Also need to consider Black’s threats. Black has a diagonal: C13 (X), D12 (O) is not X, so no. Black has a vertical: E10 (X), E11 (O) blocked, E12 (X), E13 (X) -> that’s three X’s in a row vertically? E10, E12, E13 are X, but E11 is O, so not connected. Actually E10, E12, E13 are X, but they are not consecutive rows. So no immediate four for Black either.\n\nGiven the sparse board, we should consider developing a strong position. A key principle is to play near existing stones to build potential. The cluster is around D-G, 9-13. One promising move is to play at F11. Why? It connects with F10 (O) and E11 (O) horizontally and diagonally. It also threatens to extend the diagonal three we have (F10, E11, D12) by playing at C14? Not directly. Actually F11 is not on that diagonal. Let’s see: F11 is adjacent to F10 and E11. It could create a vertical pair with F10. Also, it blocks any Black horizontal at row 1